#!/usr/bin/bash
# =============================================================================
# aquarius-session-portals — make the portals ours at the start of a login
# =============================================================================
# WHAT A PORTAL IS, IN ONE SENTENCE
#   The middleman an application talks to when it wants to record the screen or
#   open a file — one front door, several back ends behind it.
#
# WHAT THIS PROGRAM DOES, IN ONE SENTENCE
#   Stops any portal left running by the LAST desktop, so that the next one to
#   start belongs to this one.
#
# WHY THAT IS NECESSARY
#   A portal reads which desktop it is in exactly once, when it starts, and
#   never looks again. Log out of GNOME and into Aquarius and the portal that
#   is still running from before is still convinced it is in GNOME — so it
#   ignores /usr/share/xdg-desktop-portal/aquarius-portals.conf, screen
#   recording never reaches the wlroots back end, and OBS shows an empty list
#   of screens with no error anywhere.
#
#   Stopping them costs nothing. Every one is started on demand: the moment an
#   application asks for a portal, D-Bus starts a fresh one, and the fresh one
#   reads our settings.
#
# WHERE IT IS CALLED FROM
#   /usr/share/aquarius/labwc/autostart, at the end, once per login. It is a
#   separate program rather than three more lines in that file so the autostart
#   stays short and the logic sits beside its twin — the logout clean-up in
#   /usr/libexec/aquarius-session-lib.
#
# Full explanation: docs/restart/aquarius-session.md, "Switching between GNOME
# and the Aquarius Desktop".
# =============================================================================

set -uo pipefail

# shellcheck source=./aquarius-session-lib
. /usr/libexec/aquarius-session-lib

aq_session_portals_refresh
